3. String Expressions
Operator / Function	Meaning	Example result
||	Join strings	'Toy' || ' Story' → Toy Story
UPPER(s)	Capitalize all	UPPER('cars') → CARS
LOWER(s)	Lowercase all	LOWER('CARS') → cars
LENGTH(s)	Count characters	LENGTH('Up') → 2
SUBSTR(s, start, n)	Cut out part	SUBSTR('Toy Story', 1, 3) → Toy
TRIM(s)	Remove spaces at ends	TRIM(' hi ') → hi
SELECT title || ' (' || year || ')' AS title_with_year,
       UPPER(director) AS director_caps
FROM movies;
4. Comparison Expressions
Return TRUE / FALSE. Used in WHERE and HAVING.

= <> != < > <= >= IS NULL / IS NOT NULL

-- Movies longer than 100 minutes
SELECT title, length_minutes
FROM movies
WHERE length_minutes > 100;

-- Directed by John Lasseter
SELECT title, director
FROM movies
WHERE director = 'John Lasseter';

-- Not directed by John Lasseter
SELECT title, director
FROM movies
WHERE director <> 'John Lasseter';
Tip: For NULL always write IS NULL — never = NULL.

5. Logical Expressions (combine conditions)
AND, OR, NOT

   WHERE year > 2000 AND director = 'Pete Docter'
        │              │
     must BOTH       must BOTH
       be true        be true
-- Long Pete Docter movies
SELECT title, year, length_minutes
FROM movies
WHERE director = 'Pete Docter' AND length_minutes > 100;

-- Either very short or very long movies
SELECT title, length_minutes
FROM movies
WHERE length_minutes < 95 OR length_minutes > 115;
6. Special Operators
6.1 BETWEEN — range check
SELECT title, year
FROM movies
WHERE year BETWEEN 1999 AND 2005;   -- inclusive both ends
6.2 IN — matches any value in a list
SELECT title, director
FROM movies
WHERE director IN ('Pete Docter', 'Andrew Stanton');
6.3 LIKE — pattern matching
Pattern	Matches
'Toy%'	starts with Toy
'%Story%'	contains Story
'_ars'	exactly 4 chars ending in "ars" (Cars, Mars…)
'%2'	ends with 2
SELECT title
FROM movies
WHERE title LIKE '%Story%';   -- Toy Story, Toy Story 2, Toy Story 3
6.4 CASE — if/else inside SQL
SELECT title,
       CASE
         WHEN length_minutes < 100 THEN 'Short'
         WHEN length_minutes <= 115 THEN 'Medium'
         ELSE 'Long'
       END AS length_category
FROM movies;
7. Aggregate Expressions
Collapse many rows into one value: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX.

SELECT COUNT(*)                        AS total_movies,
       AVG(rating) * 10                AS avg_rating_percent,
       MAX(domestic_sales) / 1000000   AS best_domestic_millions,
       MIN(year)                       AS earliest_year
FROM movies
JOIN boxoffice ON movies.id = boxoffice.movie_id;
COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) skips NULL values.

8. Expression Precedence (order of evaluation)
   1. ( )          parentheses first — always safest
   2. *  /  %      multiply/divide/modulo
   3. +  -         add/subtract
   4. = <> < >     comparisons
   5. NOT
   6. AND
   7. OR           evaluated last
Example: 2 + 3 * 4 = 2 + 12 = 14, not 20. Use brackets to be explicit: (2 + 3) * 4 = 20.

